item.description:
We're open! Visit kó's booth at Art X Lagos, open through Sunday. 

kó's presentation includes two new paintings by Ruth Ige (b. 1993, Ilé-Ifẹ̀, Nigeria), who employs a rich palette of indigo and blue to depict enigmatic female figures suspended between portraiture and imagined landscapes. Ige drawing upon African cultural traditions and ideas of identity, spirituality, and protection. Her paintings construct mythical spaces where silhouetted figures, predominantly women, inhabit worlds that feel at once earthly and otherworldly. 

This presentation also includes new work by Abdus Salaam (b. 1989, Cape Town, South Africa), who creates abstract sculptures inspired by natural forms, exploring the relationship between materiality, spirituality, and the surrounding environment. His work incorporates a cultural fusion of Western, Eastern, and African sensibilities, meditating on time and the ethereal relationship between matter and meaning. Salaam spent his formative years on a remote South African farm without electricity, where he painted and sculpted with earth, stone, and clay from the waterhole. Rooted in his poetry and his spiritual practice, his art is both prayer and exploration, seeking unity, surrender, joy, and transcendence. In three new sculptures, Salaam worked with African Turqoise, Namibian Marble, Petrified Wood (Turkey), Quartz, and Fluorite, hand-carving the sculptures with material juxtaposition that speak to narratives of love, loss, marriage, and parenthood. 

@ruth_ige_art 
@abdussalaamart 
@artxlagos

Congratulations to Carl E. Hazlewood, Victor Davson and the Newark Museum for this feature in The New York Times. The article focuses on Dreamtime in Newark: Aljira and the Making of Global Contemporary Art, an exhibition at the Newark Museum exploring the legacy of Aljira, A Center for Contemporary Art and the community it served.  Founded by Hazlewood and Davson in the early 80s, Aljira served as a site for supporting and cultivating the careers of many artists and curators including Derrick Adams, El Anatsui, Firelei Báez, Amiri Baraka, Dawoud Bey, Frank Bowling, Jeffrey Gibson, Hew Locke, Jaune Quick-to-See Smith and Mickalene Thomas. The Newark Museum exhibition foregrounds the organization’s inclusive programming and lasting influence on contemporary art across the globe.

Read the full article, linked in bio.

@carle.hazlewood @newarkmuseumart

NYC! Tickets going fast for my Nov 16 show at 54 Below (ticket link in bio)—we’ll be kicking off my four-concert full-band residency by celebrating the release of my new EP, ‘Four Songs From Dead Outlaw,’ out the same day. More about the EP below (pre-save that too, at link in bio), and I hope you’ll join us for the concert! —DY

Following his recent multiple successes from behind the curtain, David Yazbek returns to center stage himself, releasing the unique ‘Four Songs From Dead Outlaw’ EP. These full-band recordings of beloved songs from his Tony-nominated Broadway hit Dead Outlaw offer a powerful and fresh take from Yazbek, co-writer Erik Della Penna, and distinguished producer Dean Sharenow. Now rendered by the actual recording artists behind them, these cult-classic songs have never sounded like this before.

Photo by @davedoobinin
@54below
@ecr.music.group
@erikdellapenna
@steelcutaudio
@briankilleenbass

#54below #davidyazbek #tonyawardwinner #tonywinner #deadoutlaw

A very special day at The Wolseley hotel, for the unveiling of my painting, ‘The Wolseley Piccadilly – Interior at Christmas’. ✨

I’m absolutely delighted that my painting has been chosen to feature on their limited-edition Christmas card, in collaboration with Bollinger Champagne, which also features in the painting. Such a lovely privilege and a very special start to the season!

A huge thank you to everyone at The Wolseley and Bollinger for such a wonderful day ❤️🥂🎨
